# Interactive zsh settings shared by BOTH machines.
#
# Sourced from zsh/zshrc_omarchy (Linux) and zsh/zprofile_mac (macOS). Keep
# anything machine-specific out of here -- paths, Homebrew, package managers
# and plugin locations all differ and belong in the per-machine files.

# --- C-w word boundaries --------------------------------------------------
# WORDCHARS lists the punctuation that counts as part of a word, so C-w
# (backward-kill-word) does NOT stop at those characters. Anything absent from
# the list is a boundary and C-w stops there.
#
# This was set per-machine for a long time and the two had quietly drifted:
#
#   word char on macOS only      + - @ \ ` |
#   word char on Linux only      ( ) [ ]
#   already agreed on both       ! # $ % & * ; < > ? ^ _ { } ~
#
# The macOS list looked like the more considered of the two -- it was written
# out in full rather than derived -- but it was also the OLDER one, and it
# still counted `-' as a word char. Adopting it wholesale would have silently
# undone the Oct/Nov 2026 Linux changes that deliberately made C-w stop at
# `-', then `.' and `/', then `='.
#
# So this is the INTERSECTION of the two, not either one of them. The useful
# property: every character that either machine already stopped at is still a
# stopping point, so no existing stop is lost. The only behaviour change is
# that each machine now also stops where the other one did -- macOS gains
# + - @ \ ` | and Linux gains ( ) [ ].
#
# Written out explicitly rather than subtracting from zsh's default
# (*?_-.[]~=/&;!#$%^(){}<>), so both machines land on an identical value
# regardless of what default a future zsh ships.
WORDCHARS='!#$%&*;<>?^_{}~'
